{
  "description": "Cross-language conformance for Config Bus `mcp.acl` loading. Each case supplies the JSON value that a bridge would receive from `Config.get(\"mcp.acl\")`; the bridge's acl_builder must build an ACL with the documented rule count + default_effect, or reject malformed input with the documented error substring.",
  "contract_version": "1.1",
  "test_cases": [
    {
      "id": "null_returns_no_acl",
      "description": "A null value yields no ACL.",
      "input": null,
      "expected_acl": null
    },
    {
      "id": "empty_object_returns_no_acl",
      "description": "An empty object yields no ACL (treated as no section configured).",
      "input": {},
      "expected_acl": null
    },
    {
      "id": "rules_only_default_deny",
      "description": "A rules list with no explicit default_effect defaults to deny.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "role:admin"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "sys.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_acl": {
        "rule_count": 1,
        "default_effect": "deny"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "default_allow_no_rules",
      "description": "default_effect='allow' with empty rules list yields an ACL with zero rules and allow default.",
      "input": {
        "default_effect": "allow",
        "rules": []
      },
      "expected_acl": {
        "rule_count": 0,
        "default_effect": "allow"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rule_with_description_and_conditions",
      "description": "Rules may carry description and conditions fields.",
      "input": {
        "default_effect": "deny",
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "role:admin"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "sys.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow",
            "description": "admin access",
            "conditions": {
              "identity_types": [
                "human",
                "system"
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_acl": {
        "rule_count": 1,
        "default_effect": "deny"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "multiple_rules_order_preserved",
      "description": "Multi-rule lists preserve ordering (ACL first-match-wins).",
      "input": {
        "default_effect": "deny",
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "role:admin"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "sys.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow"
          },
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "sys.reload"
            ],
            "effect": "deny"
          },
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "public.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_acl": {
        "rule_count": 3,
        "default_effect": "deny"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "approval_required_accepted",
      "description": "An `allow` rule may carry `approval: \"required\"` (apcore 0.28.0, PROTOCOL_SPEC 6.1.6, argument-scoped approval).",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "@external"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "system.control.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow",
            "approval": "required"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_acl": {
        "rule_count": 1,
        "default_effect": "deny"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "approval_not_required_accepted",
      "description": "`approval: \"not_required\"` MUST be accepted. It is spec-sanctioned (PROTOCOL_SPEC 6.1.6) and is apcore's own default for the field, so a rule carrying it loads from apcore's native `acl/` directory; a bridge that rejects it as a redundant spelling is stricter than the schema it bridges, and the identical rule then fails only on the Config Bus path. Regression pin: apcore-mcp-rust and apcore-mcp-typescript each independently narrowed this set to `required` only, which this case exists to prevent recurring.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "public.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow",
            "approval": "not_required"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_acl": {
        "rule_count": 1,
        "default_effect": "deny"
      }
    }
  ],
  "error_cases": [
    {
      "id": "invalid_default_effect",
      "description": "default_effect must be 'allow' or 'deny'.",
      "input": {
        "default_effect": "maybe",
        "rules": []
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "default_effect must be"
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_callers",
      "description": "A rule missing 'callers' is rejected.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "targets": [
              "x.*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "'callers' must be a non-empty list"
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_targets",
      "description": "A rule missing 'targets' is rejected.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "'targets' must be a non-empty list"
    },
    {
      "id": "invalid_effect",
      "description": "A rule's effect must be 'allow' or 'deny'.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "*"
            ],
            "effect": "maybe"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "'effect' must be 'allow' or 'deny'"
    },
    {
      "id": "unknown_rule_key",
      "description": "Unknown keys on a rule are rejected.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow",
            "bogus": true
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "unexpected keys"
    },
    {
      "id": "non_object_top_level",
      "description": "The top-level `mcp.acl` value must be an object.",
      "input": "deny",
      "expected_error_substring": "must be a mapping"
    },
    {
      "id": "rules_non_array",
      "description": "The `rules` field must be a list.",
      "input": {
        "rules": "oops"
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "rules must be a list"
    },
    {
      "id": "approval_unknown_value",
      "description": "A value outside the closed set {required, not_required} is rejected.",
      "input": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "callers": [
              "*"
            ],
            "targets": [
              "*"
            ],
            "effect": "allow",
            "approval": "sometimes"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_error_substring": "'approval' must be"
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "known_divergences": [
      "All three SDK ACL constructors take `rules: Vec<ACLRule> | list[ACLRule] | ACLRule[]` and a `default_effect` string. Python and TypeScript store `description` as a required string (empty when omitted); Rust stores it as Option<String> (None when omitted). Conformance cases only assert rule counts and default_effect, not individual rule-field reconstructions.",
      "The `deny` + `approval: required` rejection (PROTOCOL_SPEC 6.1.6 rule 2) is deliberately NOT a shared error_case: the rejection originates in apcore's own ACLRule/ACL constructor rather than in the bridge, and its message wording differs per language (Rust wraps it as a Config error naming `mcp.acl`; Python surfaces apcore's ACLRuleError). It is covered by a per-language test in each bridge instead."
    ]
  }
}
